  "nameStory": "Rose-of-Sharon gets its poetic name from its large, showy yellow flowers that bloom prolifically, creating a carpet of golden blooms reminiscent of the biblical \"rose of Sharon.\" The name \"Aaron's beard\" refers to how this low-growing shrub spreads across the ground with flowing, beard-like coverage.",
  "description": "Rose-of-Sharon is a low-growing, spreading shrub that hugs the ground like a living carpet. Originally from the mountains along the Black Sea, this cheerful plant has escaped gardens to naturalize in parts of New England. It rarely grows taller than a foot but can spread several feet wide, creating dense mats of evergreen foliage topped with bright golden-yellow flowers that can be as large as 3 inches across.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Flowers",
      "description": "Large bright yellow flowers with five petals and a prominent burst of golden stamens in the center, blooming from late spring through summer - much larger and showier than other St. John's worts",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Habit",
      "description": "Forms low, spreading mats that creep along the ground, staying under 12 inches tall but spreading outward like a living carpet",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Leaves",
      "description": "Evergreen oval leaves arranged in opposite pairs, feeling leathery and staying green through winter unlike most other St. John's worts",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Fruit/Seeds",
      "description": "Small brown capsules that split open to release tiny seeds, developing after the showy flowers fade",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Rose-of-Sharon in disturbed soils, abandoned garden sites, and woodland edges where it has escaped cultivation. It prefers well-drained soils and can tolerate both sun and partial shade, often persisting around old home foundations or spreading from neglected landscaping.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "New growth emerges bright green, and the first large yellow flowers begin appearing in late spring, much earlier and larger than native St. John's worts.",
    "summer": "Peak blooming period with abundant large yellow flowers that can nearly cover the plant, making identification easiest during this season.",
    "fall": "Flowers continue into early fall while seed capsules develop and split open, with evergreen leaves remaining bright green.",
    "winter": "Evergreen foliage persists through winter as low mats, making this the only St. John's wort you'll see staying green under snow."
  },
  "funFact": "This plant is such an enthusiastic spreader that a single plant can potentially cover an area the size of a small room within just a few years, earning it the nickname \"nature's living carpet\" among gardeners who both love and fear its vigorous growth.",
  "confusionNotes": "Rose-of-Sharon is easily distinguished from other St. John's worts by its much larger flowers (often 3+ inches across versus 1 inch or less for natives) and its evergreen, ground-hugging growth habit. Remember: \"Big blooms, stays green\" for Rose-of-Sharon versus smaller flowers and deciduous habits for most native species.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Don't confuse this Rose-of-Sharon with the tall flowering shrub (Hibiscus syriacus) that shares the same common name - they're completely unrelated plants. This creeping version belongs to the St. John's wort family, while the tall shrub Rose-of-Sharon is in the mallow family.",
  "compareHints": "Rose-of-Sharon stands out dramatically from its St. John's wort relatives with flowers that can be three times larger than native species like Common St. John's Wort. While native St. John's worts are typically upright herbs that die back in winter, Rose-of-Sharon stays low and evergreen year-round. Its prostrate, mat-forming growth habit is also unique among New England's St. John's worts, which tend to grow upright.",
